WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.736
A for loop repeats an instruction for each value in a

00:00:03.736 --> 00:00:07.471
sequence. Instead of writing the same instruction three times, we write

00:00:07.471 --> 00:00:11.207
it once inside a loop. Let us follow a small example.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.730
In this code, range of three supplies zero, one, and two.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:19.861
The variable i takes one value at a time. The indented

00:00:19.861 --> 00:00:23.991
print statement runs once for each value. Indentation places the statement

00:00:23.991 --> 00:00:25.117
inside the loop.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.222
The output is zero, then one, then two, each on its

00:00:29.222 --> 00:00:32.944
own line. Three is excluded because range stops before its upper

00:00:32.944 --> 00:00:36.666
limit. After two, there are no values left, so the loop

00:00:36.666 --> 00:00:37.004
ends.
